WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.559 --> 00:00:24.900
A 

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:30.730
tracheoesophageal fistula is a birth defect
in which the esophagus has an abnormal connection

00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:32.640
to the trachea.

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:38.489
The esophagus is the tube that food passes
through from the mouth to the stomach, and

00:00:38.489 --> 00:00:44.650
the trachea is the windpipe that air passes
through from the mouth and nose to the lungs.

00:00:44.650 --> 00:00:50.310
The trachea forms during the sixth week of
pregnancy.

00:00:50.310 --> 00:00:58.010
Here, you can see the tube that will eventually
form all the organs of the digestive system.

00:00:58.010 --> 00:01:03.809
The trachea and lungs grow from the part of
the digestive tube that will eventually become

00:01:03.809 --> 00:01:08.200
the esophagus.

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:13.869
For unknown reasons, the esophagus and trachea
may grow and separate abnormally during this

00:01:13.869 --> 00:01:15.530
time.

00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:21.439
The esophagus may end in a blind pouch with
missing gaps and be abnormally narrow.

00:01:21.439 --> 00:01:27.299
This absence or narrowing of a natural body
passageway is called atresia.

00:01:27.299 --> 00:01:33.020
The esophagus and trachea may also have an
abnormal connection called a tracheoesophageal

00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:35.109
fistula.

00:01:35.109 --> 00:01:43.469
Here, we see the most common type of tracheoesophageal
fistula in a newborn infant.

00:01:43.469 --> 00:01:50.630
The upper esophagus ends in a blind pouch,
and the lower esophagus connects to the trachea.

00:01:50.630 --> 00:01:57.259
This is a serious problem because stomach
contents can travel up the esophagus and pass

00:01:57.259 --> 00:02:03.270
through the fistula into the trachea and lungs.

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:08.350
The fistula can also cause difficulty breathing
for the newborn since air can now bypass the

00:02:08.350 --> 00:02:10.729
lungs and enter the stomach.

00:02:10.729 --> 00:02:18.819
Before the tracheoesophageal fistula repair
procedure, an intravenous line will be started.

00:02:18.819 --> 00:02:24.709
The baby may be given antibiotics through
the IV to decrease the chance of infection.

00:02:24.709 --> 00:02:30.310
The baby will be given general anesthesia
which will put the baby to sleep for the entire

00:02:30.310 --> 00:02:31.310
operation.

00:02:31.310 --> 00:02:36.150
A breathing tube will be inserted through
the mouth and down the throat to help the

00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:39.739
baby breathe during the operation.

00:02:39.739 --> 00:02:47.579
The surgeon will make an incision in the baby’s
chest, usually on the right side.

00:02:47.579 --> 00:02:52.540
Through the incision, the surgeon will gently
move the lungs aside to view the trachea and

00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:55.720
the esophagus.

00:02:55.720 --> 00:03:01.769
After identifying the tracheoesophageal fistula,
the surgeon will slowly close the fistula’s

00:03:01.769 --> 00:03:07.329
connection with the trachea with sutures,
then cut the connection away from the trachea.

00:03:07.329 --> 00:03:13.040
The fistula’s connection with the esophagus
will also be cut, and the fistula will be

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:15.939
removed.

00:03:15.939 --> 00:03:24.010
Next, the surgeon will make an incision at
the end of the upper esophagus to open it.

00:03:24.010 --> 00:03:31.590
Then, the upper and lower esophagus will be
connected with sutures.

00:03:31.590 --> 00:03:39.319
Finally, the surgeon will insert a surgical
drain in the chest and close the incision

00:03:39.319 --> 00:03:44.280
with sutures.

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:48.310
After the procedure, the baby will continue
to use the breathing tube until they’ve

00:03:48.310 --> 00:03:50.780
healed enough to breathe on their own.

00:03:50.780 --> 00:03:56.819
The baby will be taken to the neonatal intensive
care unit for monitoring.

00:03:56.819 --> 00:03:58.659
Pain medication will be given.

00:03:58.659 --> 00:04:03.700
The baby may continue to receive antibiotics
through the IV.

00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:08.349
Babies are released from the hospital when
they’re able to eat enough to maintain their

00:04:08.349 --> 00:04:11.510
weight, which may be after two weeks or longer.

